const page = { page: "lesson", paper: "paper-1", topic: "topic-2", unit: "2-1", lesson: "makki-madani-surahs" };
const title = "Makki and Madani Surahs · 2.1 · IGCSE Islamiyat";
const footerHtml = "Cambridge IGCSE Islamiyat 0493 &middot; Paper 1, Topic 2, Unit 2.1";
const mainHtml = "<section class=\"chapter\">\r\n      <div class=\"wrap\">\r\n        <div class=\"chapter-head\">\r\n          <h1>Makki and Madani Surahs</h1>\r\n          <p class=\"ch-sub\">2.1 The Revelation of the Qur&rsquo;an &middot; classifying every surah by when it was revealed</p>\r\n        </div>\r\n\r\n        <p class=\"lede prose-col\">Two surahs can sound like they belong to different books: one thunders in short, urgent verses about the sun going dark and the stars falling; another calmly lays out, over many long verses, exactly how a family&rsquo;s inheritance should be divided. They are not from different books &mdash; they are from different <em>moments</em> in the same twenty-three-year revelation, and learning to tell those moments apart is what this lesson is about.</p>\r\n\r\n        <div class=\"box box-predict\">\r\n          <p class=\"box-label\">&#9633; Predict before you read</p>\r\n          <p>Before you read on: which of the two surahs described above &mdash; the short, urgent one about cosmic signs, or the long, calm one about inheritance law &mdash; do you think came <em>first</em> in the Prophet&rsquo;s mission, and why might the order matter for how a Muslim reads either one today? Hold onto your answer; you&rsquo;ll test it against the actual pattern by the end of the lesson.</p>\r\n        </div>\r\n\r\n        <h3 style=\"max-width:66ch; margin:40px auto 4px;\" class=\"prose-col\">One revelation, two eras</h3>\r\n        <p class=\"prose-col\" style=\"margin-top:14px;\">Scholars of the Qur&rsquo;an divide its 114 surahs (and, more precisely, its individual verses) into two broad categories: <strong>Makki</strong> (Makkan) and <strong>Madani</strong> (Madinan). The obvious guess is that this is simply about geography &mdash; which city the words came down in. It isn&rsquo;t, quite, and getting that distinction right is worth marks on its own.</p>\r\n\r\n        <h3 style=\"max-width:66ch; margin:40px auto 4px;\" class=\"prose-col\">The definition scholars actually use</h3>\r\n        <p class=\"prose-col\" style=\"margin-top:14px;\">The standard, majority definition used by scholars of <em>&rsquo;Uloom al-Qur&rsquo;aan</em> (&ldquo;the sciences of the Qur&rsquo;an&rdquo;) is <strong>chronological, not geographical</strong>: a verse is <strong>Makki</strong> if it was revealed at any point <em>before</em> the Prophet&rsquo;s migration (<strong>Hijrah</strong>) to Madinah in 622 CE, and <strong>Madani</strong> if it was revealed <em>after</em> the Hijrah &mdash; regardless of where in the world the Prophet physically was standing when it came down. Some scholars have proposed alternative definitions &mdash; classifying by the actual place of revelation, or by which audience a verse addresses &mdash; but these run into real problems: plenty of &ldquo;Makki-era&rdquo; verses were revealed on journeys away from Makkah (for instance during the Hijrah itself), and, more strikingly, entire surahs revealed <em>inside or near</em> Makkah years after the Hijrah &mdash; such as <strong>Surah al-Fath</strong>, revealed near Hudaybiyyah when the Prophet returned toward Makkah in 6 AH &mdash; are still classified as Madani, because by then the Hijrah had already happened. Chronology, not location, is what actually does the classifying.</p>\r\n\r\n        <h3 style=\"max-width:66ch; margin:40px auto 4px;\" class=\"prose-col\">How scholars actually know which is which</h3>\r\n        <p class=\"prose-col\" style=\"margin-top:14px;\">Given that this classification depends on timing rather than an obvious label attached to each verse, how did scholars establish it at all? Two methods:</p>\r\n\r\n        <div class=\"steps\">\r\n          <div class=\"step\">\r\n            <span class=\"n\">1</span>\r\n            <div><h4>Transmitted testimony (naql)</h4><p>Direct reports from the Companions and the earliest generation of Muslims, who had witnessed the circumstances of a verse&rsquo;s revelation firsthand and passed that information down.</p></div>\r\n          </div>\r\n          <div class=\"step\">\r\n            <span class=\"n\">2</span>\r\n            <div><h4>Deduction from internal evidence (ijtihaad)</h4><p>Where no direct testimony survived, scholars examined a verse&rsquo;s language, themes, and the historical situation it responds to, and inferred its period from recognisable internal patterns &mdash; the same patterns that let students today learn to recognise Makki and Madani style on sight.</p></div>\r\n          </div>\r\n        </div>\r\n\r\n        <div class=\"pull\">\r\n          <span class=\"translation\">&ldquo;By Time. Indeed, mankind is in loss, except for those who believe and do righteous deeds and advise each other to truth and advise each other to patience.&rdquo;</span>\r\n          <span class=\"cite\">Qur&rsquo;an 103:1&ndash;3 (Surah al-&rsquo;Asr) &mdash; a short, oath-opened, Makki-style surah</span>\r\n        </div>\r\n\r\n        <div class=\"pull\">\r\n          <span class=\"translation\">&ldquo;Allah instructs you concerning your children: for the male, what is equal to the share of two females&hellip;&rdquo;</span>\r\n          <span class=\"cite\">Qur&rsquo;an 4:11 (Surah an-Nisa) &mdash; a long, detailed, Madani-style legal ruling</span>\r\n        </div>\r\n\r\n        <div class=\"compare\">\r\n          <div>\r\n            <h4>Makki verses &mdash; the earlier, Makkan-period style</h4>\r\n            <ul>\r\n              <li><strong>Themes:</strong> establishing core belief &mdash; the Oneness of God (tawhid), the reality of the afterlife (Paradise and Hell), and stories of earlier prophets and the nations that rejected them.</li>\r\n              <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> confronting the polytheism of the Quraysh and calling individuals to personal faith, before any Muslim community or state existed to legislate for.</li>\r\n              <li><strong>Style:</strong> generally shorter surahs and verses, frequent oaths (&ldquo;By the sun&hellip; by the moon&hellip;&rdquo;), vivid, urgent, often poetic language, and heavy repetition for emphasis.</li>\r\n              <li><strong>Address:</strong> often opens with the universal &ldquo;O mankind&rdquo; (<em>yaa ayyuhan-naas</em>), since the audience was still the whole unconverted city, not an established believing community.</li>\r\n              <li><strong>Disjointed letters:</strong> the mysterious opening letters (<em>al-huroof al-muqatta&rsquo;ah</em>, e.g. Alif-Laam-Meem) appear at the start of 29 surahs &mdash; all but two of which are Makki.</li>\r\n              <li><strong>Sajdah verses:</strong> most of the Qur&rsquo;an&rsquo;s recognised prostration verses occur in Makki surahs.</li>\r\n            </ul>\r\n          </div>\r\n          <div>\r\n            <h4>Madani verses &mdash; the later, Madinan-period style</h4>\r\n            <ul>\r\n              <li><strong>Themes:</strong> practical legislation &mdash; family law, inheritance, commerce, criminal penalties, the rules of treaties and warfare, and guidance for governing an actual, growing community.</li>\r\n              <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> organising and regulating a Muslim society that now existed, including its relationships with hypocrites (<em>munafiqoon</em>) within Madinah and Jewish and Christian communities nearby.</li>\r\n              <li><strong>Style:</strong> generally longer surahs and verses, more measured, legally precise language, with detailed conditions and exceptions spelled out.</li>\r\n              <li><strong>Address:</strong> characteristically opens with &ldquo;O you who believe&rdquo; (<em>yaa ayyuhal-ladheena aamanoo</em>), addressing an already-established community of believers directly.</li>\r\n              <li><strong>Disjointed letters:</strong> only two of the 29 surahs that open with disjointed letters are Madani &mdash; Surah al-Baqarah and Surah Aal-&rsquo;Imran &mdash; a well-known exception worth remembering precisely because it breaks the pattern.</li>\r\n              <li><strong>Length pattern:</strong> although Madani surahs are far fewer in number (roughly a quarter of the total), they make up a large share of the Qur&rsquo;an&rsquo;s total length, because they run so much longer verse-for-verse.</li>\r\n            </ul>\r\n          </div>\r\n        </div>\r\n\r\n        <h3 style=\"max-width:66ch; margin:40px auto 4px;\" class=\"prose-col\">Categories, not just a two-way split</h3>\r\n        <p class=\"prose-col\" style=\"margin-top:14px;\">Because the Qur&rsquo;an as a finished book is arranged by length and theme rather than by the order verses were revealed, individual surahs don&rsquo;t always fall neatly into one category. Scholars recognise three categories:</p>\r\n\r\n        <div class=\"steps\">\r\n          <div class=\"step\">\r\n            <span class=\"n\">1</span>\r\n            <div><h4>Purely Makki</h4><p>Surahs revealed in their entirety before the Hijrah, with no later material added, such as Surah al-&rsquo;Alaq (96), the surah of the first revelation, and Surah al-Fatiha.</p></div>\r\n          </div>\r\n          <div class=\"step\">\r\n            <span class=\"n\">2</span>\r\n            <div><h4>Purely Madani</h4><p>Surahs revealed in their entirety after the Hijrah, such as Surah al-Baqarah, the Qur&rsquo;an&rsquo;s longest surah and a dense collection of Madinan-period law.</p></div>\r\n          </div>\r\n          <div class=\"step\">\r\n            <span class=\"n\">3</span>\r\n            <div><h4>Mixed surahs</h4><p>Surahs that, as they appear in the mushaf today, combine verses from both periods; a surah&rsquo;s overall classification is then usually decided by whichever period contributed the majority of its verses, or by where its opening verses were revealed.</p></div>\r\n          </div>\r\n        </div>\r\n\r\n        <h3 style=\"max-width:66ch; margin:40px auto 4px;\" class=\"prose-col\">Why the mushaf&rsquo;s order doesn&rsquo;t tell you the story</h3>\r\n        <p class=\"prose-col\" style=\"margin-top:14px;\">It&rsquo;s worth stating plainly, because it trips students up: the order surahs appear in the Qur&rsquo;an today has <strong>nothing to do with the order they were revealed in</strong>. Surah al-Baqarah, for example, sits second in the mushaf but was revealed years into the Madinan period; Surah al-&rsquo;Alaq, which contains the very first words ever revealed, sits 96th. Knowing a surah&rsquo;s Makki/Madani classification is one of the main tools scholars use to reconstruct the <em>actual</em> chronological sequence of revelation, separate from the book&rsquo;s final, thematic arrangement.</p>\r\n\r\n        <h3 style=\"max-width:66ch; margin:40px auto 4px;\" class=\"prose-col\">Why any of this earns marks</h3>\r\n\r\n        <div class=\"steps\">\r\n          <div class=\"step\">\r\n            <span class=\"n\">1</span>\r\n            <div><h4>Context for interpretation</h4><p>Knowing whether a verse addresses new believers in a hostile city or an established community in Madinah stops a student from misreading who a verse is actually speaking to.</p></div>\r\n          </div>\r\n          <div class=\"step\">\r\n            <span class=\"n\">2</span>\r\n            <div><h4>Explains the shift from belief to law</h4><p>It shows <em>why</em> the Qur&rsquo;an spends its earlier years on faith and its later years on legislation: you cannot sensibly legislate inheritance or criminal penalties for a community that doesn&rsquo;t exist yet and doesn&rsquo;t yet believe in God.</p></div>\r\n          </div>\r\n          <div class=\"step\">\r\n            <span class=\"n\">3</span>\r\n            <div><h4>Helps resolve apparent contradictions</h4><p>Where two verses seem to give different guidance on the same subject, knowing which came first (Makki or Madani, and roughly when within each period) is the essential first step in understanding abrogation (<em>naskh</em>) and the refinement of rulings over time.</p></div>\r\n          </div>\r\n          <div class=\"step\">\r\n            <span class=\"n\">4</span>\r\n            <div><h4>Corroborates the historical record</h4><p>The internal evidence within the verses themselves (their themes, their audience, their concerns) independently matches the outside historical account of the Prophet&rsquo;s life, reinforcing that the Qur&rsquo;an&rsquo;s text and Islamic history describe the same real events.</p></div>\r\n          </div>\r\n          <div class=\"step\">\r\n            <span class=\"n\">5</span>\r\n            <div><h4>Strengthens exactly the kind of AO2 answer this syllabus rewards</h4><p>See the Source Box below on exam coverage &mdash; this lesson supplies depth material for a question type already set twice on this syllabus.</p></div>\r\n          </div>\r\n        </div>\r\n\r\n        <div class=\"box box-source\">\r\n          <p class=\"box-label\">&#9633; Where this comes from</p>\r\n          <p>This lesson follows the structure of Chapter 6, &ldquo;The Makkee and the Madanee Verses,&rdquo; in Abu Ammaar Yasir Qadhi&rsquo;s academic study <em>An Introduction to the Sciences of the Qur&rsquo;aan</em> (Al-Hidaayah, 1999) &mdash; specifically its four sections: the Definition of Makkee and Madanee, the Knowledge of Makkee and Madanee Verses, the Attributes (common themes and specific characteristics) of Makkee and Madanee Revelations, the Categories of Makkee and Madanee, and the Benefits of Knowing Makkee and Madanee verses. Qur&rsquo;an citations are given in standard English translation. Specific example surahs (al-Fath, al-&rsquo;Alaq, al-Fatiha, al-Baqarah) and their classifications are well-established and cross-checked against standard <em>&rsquo;Uloom al-Qur&rsquo;aan</em> scholarship (e.g. al-Suyuti&rsquo;s <em>al-Itqan fi &rsquo;Uloom al-Qur&rsquo;aan</em>), which is the same broader scholarly tradition Qadhi&rsquo;s book draws on and cites throughout this chapter.</p>\r\n        </div>\r\n\r\n        <div class=\"box box-source\">\r\n          <p class=\"box-label\">&#9633; Common mix-up to avoid &mdash; &ldquo;Makki&rdquo; does not mean &ldquo;revealed in Makkah&rdquo;</p>\r\n          <p>It&rsquo;s tempting to assume the names are purely geographic, but the standard definition is chronological (before/after the Hijrah), not locational. The clearest proof is Surah al-Fath: revealed near Hudaybiyyah, right outside Makkah, in the sixth year after the Hijrah &mdash; geographically about as &ldquo;Makkan&rdquo; as a revelation gets &mdash; yet it is classified as <strong>Madani</strong>, because it came after the migration. If an exam-style question ever asks you to justify a surah&rsquo;s classification, reach for &ldquo;before or after the Hijrah,&rdquo; never &ldquo;where the Prophet happened to be standing.&rdquo;</p>\r\n        </div>\r\n\r\n        <div class=\"box box-elaborate\">\r\n          <p class=\"box-label\">&#9633; Elaborate &mdash; revisit your prediction</p>\r\n          <p>Revisit your Predict Box guess. The short, oath-driven surah about cosmic signs is Makki-style; the long, calm surah about inheritance law is Madani-style. Now explain, in your own words, <em>why</em> the order had to run this way round &mdash; why it would have made little sense for a community that didn&rsquo;t yet exist and didn&rsquo;t yet believe in one God to receive detailed inheritance law before it received the case for God&rsquo;s existence at all.</p>\r\n        </div>\r\n\r\n        <div class=\"box box-elaborate\">\r\n          <p class=\"box-label\">&#9633; Elaborate &mdash; write an AO2 answer</p>\r\n          <p>This lesson connects directly to a question this syllabus has already asked twice: &ldquo;Why was it important for revelations to come over time?&rdquo; (J23 s12) and &ldquo;Significance of the Qur&rsquo;an being revealed over 23 years&rdquo; (J22 s12). Using only what you&rsquo;ve learned in this lesson &mdash; not the Cave of Hira narrative from the previous lesson &mdash; write a full AO2 answer (aim for 3&ndash;4 developed points) to one of those two questions, built entirely around the shift from Makki themes (belief) to Madani themes (law and community).</p>\r\n        </div>\r\n\r\n        <div class=\"box box-source\">\r\n          <p class=\"box-label\">&#9633; A note on exam coverage</p>\r\n          <p>Makki/Madani classification isn&rsquo;t listed as its own separate bullet point in the 0493 syllabus, and no past paper in this project&rsquo;s question bank asks for it directly as a part (a) question. What it <em>does</em> do is supply the strongest possible AO2 material for a question type that has already been set twice on this exact syllabus point &mdash; &ldquo;why did revelation come gradually, over 23 years?&rdquo; (J22 s12, J23 s12 &mdash; see the Exam Lab in the &ldquo;First Revelation&rdquo; lesson for their full part (a) content). Treat this lesson as your depth material for that (b) part, not as a topic with its own dedicated past-paper question.</p>\r\n        </div>\r\n\r\n        <div style=\"max-width:66ch; margin:40px auto 0;\" class=\"prose-col\">\r\n          <h3 style=\"display:flex; align-items:baseline; gap:12px; flex-wrap:wrap;\">Ready to practise?</h3>\r\n          <p style=\"margin-top:10px; color:var(--ink-soft);\">A short Practice Quiz first, then a graded Quiz once you're confident.</p>\r\n        </div>\r\n\r\n        <div class=\"lesson-pager\">\r\n          <a class=\"pager-link\" href=\"../why-gradual-revelation/quiz.html\"><span class=\"pg-label\">&larr; Back</span><div class=\"pg-title\">Why Gradual Revelation Quiz</div></a>\r\n          <a class=\"pager-link next\" href=\"practice.html\"><span class=\"pg-label\">Next &rarr;</span><div class=\"pg-title\">Practice Quiz</div></a>\r\n        </div>\r\n      </div>\r\n    </section>";
